## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design offline-capable telemetry at the edge with guaranteed delivery and strict tenancy isolation.

## Key Concepts

- Edge buffering, per-tenant isolation, idempotent writes
- Out-of-order handling and replay-safe semantics
- Pluggable sinks and backpressure-aware flushing
- Testing offline durability and cross-tenant isolation

## Code Example

```javascript
class EdgeBuffer {
  constructor() { this.store = new Map(); }
  add(event) {
    const key = `${event.tenant_id}:${event.seq}`;
    this.store.set(key, event);
  }
  flush(toSink) { /* dedupe by (tenant_id, seq) and send in order */ }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement exactly-once semantics across edge and cloud sinks?
- How do you test failure modes like prolonged offline periods and partial flushes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design resilient, scalable OTCA pipelines that cope with edge intermittency and cross-region data flight. It emphasizes practical consistency, privacy, and schema strategy under real-world constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Edge buffering and bounded memory
- Per-tenant privacy masking
- Deduplication and idempotent writes
- Avro schema evolution (backward/forward compat)
- Region-aware routing and cross-region aggregation
- Backpressure and QoS controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo dedupe check (edge/local)
function isDuplicate(event, seen) {
  const key = `${event.tenant_id}:${event.event_id}`;
  const last = seen.get(key) || -Infinity;
  if (event.ts <= last) return true;
  seen.set(key, event.ts);
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate cross-region reconciliation latency?
- What changes if edge buffers exceed memory under bursty traffic?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of data governance in OTCA, practical ingestion-time policy enforcement, and real-world routing/partitioning decisions for privacy.

## Key Concepts
- Data classification and ingestion-time policy
- Per-tenant privacy controls and lineage
- Streaming routing to separate topics
- Parquet partitioning and audit trails

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple policy lookup
function decideAction(tenantId, category) {
  // naive rule: sensitive_financial always redact unless tenant whitelisted
  const policies = { 'sensitive_financial': { redact: true, whitelist: [] } };
  const c = policies[category];
  return c && c.redact ? 'redact' : 'allow';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the policy engine's behavior across tenants with differing policies?
- How would you implement schema evolution for payload while ensuring redaction doesn't break downstream consumers?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design privacy-first OTCA paths that enforce data residency, tenant isolation, and masking, going beyond basic streaming.

## Key Concepts

- OTCA data contracts, regional immutable storage, global masked aggregates
- Per-tenant masking (tokenization, redaction) and tenant-key management
- Data lineage, access controls, and residency verification

## Code Example

```javascript
function maskRecord(record, tenantKey) {
  const masked = { ...record };
  if (masked.email) masked.email = hash(masked.email + tenantKey);
  if (masked.phone) masked.phone = redact(masked.phone);
  // redact other PII fields as needed
  return masked;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test masking correctness and residency under synthetic bursts?
- How do you handle schema evolution for masked fields across regions?

